  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\n- Isolated showers this afternoon, then dry through late Tuesday\n\nThe larger area of deformation zone precipitation has exited the \nforecast area. Radar shows isolated showers across the eastern CWA\nmoving southwest, mainly around US-127. Some model guidance, \nparticularly the NBM, favors thunder this afternoon with this \nactivity in the Lansing area. Given limited conditional instability\nthat is mostly below the ice production layer, we feel that \nthunder is quite unlikely, but admittedly non-zero, with this \nactivity.\n\nAfter this, forecast expectations have really not changed for the \nweek ahead. There is a signal for some Monday morning shallow ground \nfog, particularly towards I-94. Otherwise, Monday through Tuesday \nafternoon feature a high certainty (greater than 90 percent) of dry \nconditions across southwest Lower MI with seasonal temperatures and \nhumidities. Model guidance run to run consistency has been decent \nwith respect to bringing precipitation chances to at least the \nnorthwest forecast area (including Ludington) Tuesday night. \nAccordingly, the latest available SPC SWODY3 brings a general \nthunder threat to the extreme northwest CWA during this timeframe.\n\n\n- Showers and storms possible from Wednesday through the weekend\n\nAs previously noted, we will remain in a persistent broad upper \ntrough pattern starting the middle of the coming week and lasting \nthrough the weekend. Guidance is locking into a slow cold frontal \npassage Wednesday into Thursday with attendant showers and \nthunderstorms. Neither SPC nor Machine Learning (ML) guidance \nindicate much of a severe threat with this activity. We are going \nwith a possible dry period Friday in the deterministic forecast, but \nthis may be a bit misleading given that numerous members from the \nECMWF/GEFS/GEPS model suites give at least a smattering of \nprecipitation every day into early next week. The ensemble guidance\noutput really speaks to the poor timing and magnitude predictability\nof this pattern given that episodic rainfall will depend greatly \non the evolution of meso-alpha scale upper PV maxima moving through\nthe broader cyclonic flow.\n\nIn terms of severe convective threats after Thursday, the ML guidance\nis not as bullish as before. This makes sense given that we are looking\nat an unsettled pattern that will probably be unable to develop significant\nconditional instability.",
